    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d109">

                <pb facs="0317" n="287" xml:id="pg287"/>

                <head>109. Telegram From the Embassy in Poland to the Department of State<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d109-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                ><placeName>Warsaw</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                                when="1956-10-25T13:00:00+01:00">October 25, 1956—1
                        p.m.</date></hi></dateline></opener>
                <p>527. For Under Secretary Hoover from Ambassador Jacobs. Embtel 526.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d109-f2"/> Following is text (a’s and the’s omitted) which I shall
                    read to Under Secretary Winiewicz at 3 p.m. this afternoon: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p><hi rendition="#italic">Begin verbal text:</hi>
                            <list>
                                <label>1.</label>
                                <item>President Eisenhower in recent address<anchor n="3"
                                        xml:id="d109-f3"/> said that US as nation has job and
                                    mission to do in helping freedom-loving peoples who need and
                                    want and can profitably use US aid so that they may advance in
                                    their ability for self-support and may add strength to security
                                    and peace of world.</item>
                                <label>2.</label>
                                <item>US authorities have noted statement which Mr. Wladyslaw
                                    Gomulka has made in recent addresses and, in particular, those
                                    relating to difficulties in Polish economic situation.<anchor
                                        n="4" xml:id="d109-f4"/> In view of these statements, US
                                    Government has been studying question of how US might be of
                                    assistance. It would be glad to have, therefore, any views which
                                    Government of Poland may have on this subject, including perhaps
                                    possibility of US supplying certain commodities, such as wheat,
                                    which it has been supplying to various other European
                                    countries.</item>
                                <label>3.</label>
                                <item>US authorities do not wish to press this matter upon
                                    Government of Poland and approach is being made at this time
                                    only from point of view of desiring to be helpful if Polish
                                    Government is interested. US Government has no fixed ideas as to
                                    procedure in event Government of Poland desires to pursue matter
                                    further.</item>
                                <label>4.</label>
                                <item>Under Secretary of State, Herbert Hoover, Jr., has great
                                    personal interest in this matter in view of association of his
                                    illustrious father, Herbert Hoover, with previous difficult
                                    situations in Polish history,<anchor n="5" xml:id="d109-f5"/> as
                                    well as his own great respect for Polish dedication to
                                    independence and freedom.</item>
                            </list></p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Jacobs</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d109-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d109-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 611.48/10–2556. Confidential; Niact;
                    Limit Distribution.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d109-f2" n="2" xml:id="d109-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 526 from Warsaw, October 25, Jacobs indicated that he was going to
                    see Winiewicz because Foreign Minister Adam Rapacki was unavailable. (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ibid</hi>.) Telegram 208 to Warsaw, October 24,
                    instructed Jacobs to make such an approach. (<hi rendition="#italic">Ibid</hi>.,
                    611.48/10–2456)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d109-f3" n="3" xml:id="d109-f3-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d99">Document 99</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d109-f4" n="4" xml:id="d109-f4-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d95">Document 95</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d109-f5" n="5" xml:id="d109-f5-content">
                <p>As Chairman of the Famine Emergency Committee, Herbert Hoover visited Poland in
                    March 1946. Hoover also served as Food Administrator during World War I.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
